    "body": "Raised to P1 after the review of PR #158 (#117): https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/pull/158#issuecomment-6713736061.\n\nThe common case is blocked: a real turned bar with **one** modelled cross hole leans the axis fit about 0.001 mm, past the spindle law's 0.0005 mm, so the spindle law refuses it, on a lathe and on a mill-turn. Since #117, leaving the hole out of the model is refused too, correctly: it would drill a hole the part doesn't have. So a single cross hole in a round bar can't be drilled today. It fails closed, so it's not a gouge.\n\nDone means (added): pin a round bar with one modelled cross hole (no mirror hole). The axis fit must read the bar's true axis within the spindle tolerance, e.g. by excluding facets that aren't surfaces of revolution (hole walls, flats, keyways) from the fit, and the cross drill must generate clean. The `tests/punched_bar` fixture's mirror holes (added in #117 as a workaround) are dropped once this lands. Related: #113 (large keyway), #152 (short bars).",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
